    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Granada grew from an Iberian oppidum and the Roman town of Iliberis, and in the 11th century the Zirids made the former Jewish town of Ġarnāṭa the main city of the region in place of Ilbīra.</li><li>From 1228 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid dynasty</a> ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Granada from the city, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> on the Sabika hill, and held out as the last Muslim-ruled state in the peninsula until 2 January 1492.</li><li>After the conquest the Muslim population was forced to convert or leave, the mosques became churches, and the city took on a Catholic and Castilian character under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and his successors.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1812 French troops leaving Granada tried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dynamite">dynamite</a> the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> and blew up eight towers before the Spanish soldier José Garcia disabled the remaining fuses.</p></aside>
    </section>

